{"title":"From frontline to workforce: co-integration as Ukraine's competitive advantage","description":"Ukraine is doing what no European country has done since 1945: reintegrating millions of civilians who became soldiers - teachers, engineers, farmers. Veteran unemployment exceeds 30%. The barrier is not skill - it is translation and trust. What does it take to build the bridge from both sides? ","extendedDescription":"Once full demobilisation begins, Ukraine will have over 5 million veterans - up to 15% of the population. It is the only European country to have experienced full-scale war on its own soil since 1945, and the only one whose army consists substantially of mobilised civilians. Existing models were not built for this. 

This panel introduces co-integration: sustainable reintegration requires transformation on both sides - veterans learning to translate their experience, employers and institutions learning to recognise it. Reintegration asks veterans to adapt. Co-integration asks society to meet them halfway. 

